WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:09.440
We are here today with Professor Allen Kachalia, and Allen is a physician, practicing internist

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.040
at the Brigham, he's also a lawyer.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.539
And, we are going to talk about the law, and regulation,

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:22.100
and how these tools are used to help assure and improve health care quality.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.140
Allen, welcome, thank you so much for coming.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.230
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.910
So Allen, I want to start off sort of taking a big step back,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.220
and we're going to begin by thinking about the legal and regulatory structure in the

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.220
US.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.310
What role does the law play in assuring that when I go

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:49.240
see a doctor, when somebody comes to a hospital, that the quality of that health care is at

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.990
least halfway good?

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.430
What can the law do in that respect?

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.780
I think there's actually three ways that the law helps

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.440
assure that we have high quality or care in this country.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.020
The first is regulation, states or the federal government

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.330
sets minimum standards for which a practitioner must meet,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.420
or a hospital must meet, if it wants to operate.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.740
The second is the liability system malpractice, that's a system of accountability and we could

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.149
talk more about that in a second.

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:21.520
And then the third is when it actually creates financial incentives, or penalties, for hospitals

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.579
and physicians and other providers, with regard to how they

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:28.630
behave and what they do, and the ACA is a classic example of that.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:32.409
So they're all really important, let's take each one of them one at a time,

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:33.570
if you don't mind.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.359
Let's start with regulation, what do you mean regulation?

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:41.549
Give me a couple of examples of the kinds of things that we do in the US

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:45.820
on regulation, and what do they have to do with quality?

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.219
I think the classic example of regulation that people think of

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:50.780
is, you need to have a license to practice.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:54.740
I think the idea there is that, if a doctor is going to practice medicine,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.420
he or she needs to have met minimum training requirements,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.520
so the idea is when people are out there practicing, we know they at least have a floor, or baseline

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.850
level, of knowledge that everybody will meet.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.329
Who sets those standards of what is a minimum level of baseline?

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:09.770
It can vary in how they're set.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.470
Sometimes they're set by the states themselves, or sometimes they're set by the states working

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.720
with the relevant kind of professional body, working with them to figure out what the standard

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.620
should look like.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.010
So the state says, if you're going to be a practicing internist,

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:28.210
or going to be a physician, in the state of Massachusetts, in the United States,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.420
you need to have what?

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.480
Gone to medical school, done a certain amount of post medical school training?

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.900
Correct, I believe in most states, it's either one or two years.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:37.730
You actually don't have to finish a full residency to do it,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.500
I think, in some states it's literally one year,

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.910
and then you can go and get a license to practice.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.780
Who are other professionals in health care, beyond physicians,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.580
who end up usually having to meet a licensing requirement?

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.720
It can include nurses, pharmacists physical therapists.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.080
I think those are all examples of people who need to have a license,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.360
or pass some kind of certification, before they can practice.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.950
Got it, so almost everybody who touches a patient, in the US, in most states,

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.880
maybe all states, you need some sort of a license

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.570
before you can get involved in patient care.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:15.150
We've talked about licensing as one set of regulatory mechanisms.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.010
What else is there from a regulatory kind of thing?

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.700
What about for hospitals?

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.020
What about for others?

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.370
For hospitals, there are regulations that are set up around,

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:30.290
fire safety for example, is subject to regulation, or how safe the building is, it up to codes?

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.080
So it won't come down on people, so all of that is subject to regulation.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.850
Just as is the air flow pattern in health care institutions,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.460
because sometimes you need isolation precautions and things like that.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.470
All of that is subject to regulation.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.730
In some states, things like nursing staffing ratios

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.510
are also subject to regulation.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:51.460
If you look at hospitals in America, if I wanted tomorrow,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.870
to go and build a hospital, set it up, and run

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:57.250
it to take care of patients, what specific to health care,

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.900
would I have to do to make sure I could run a hospital in Boston tomorrow?

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.580
That's a great question, and I'm not sure I know exactly

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.270
all the people you'd have to interact with the make this happen.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.640
I imagine you'd have to work at least with the Department of Public Health,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.840
because they also issue regulations around what hospitals need to do.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.310
OK, so we have Department of Public Health, what about regulators

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.350
sort of beyond?

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:20.199
Things like Medicare, Medicare is the biggest payer

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:22.259
of health care in America.

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:24.990
Do they get involved in figuring out like what hospitals their patients

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.770
can go to, or can a Medicare patient go to any hospital he or she wants?

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.870
One, Medicare does get involved in regulation.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.520
For you to receive payment from the federal government for Medicare

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.710
patients, there are actually what they call conditions of participation

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.800
that you have to meet.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.479
One of the common conditions of participation that people talk about

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:46.639
is, you either have to be reviewed by the government, accredited

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:48.960
by the government, so they can say you are up to snuff, with regard

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.669
to all the standards they've set.

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:53.990
Or you can undergo what's called joint commission accreditation, where

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:57.680
the joint commission comes in and reviews you.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.949
I think as long as you meet all of Medicares conditions of participation,

00:05:01.949 --> 00:05:05.349
Medicare patients can come in to pretty much any hospital that they want,

00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:07.120
and the government will pay.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.830
There are certain regulations that can constrain the flow of patients,

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.740
but those don't have to do with regulation on the hospital end,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.560
they have to do with it on the patient end.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.090
Because some patients can sign up for either what they call traditional Medicare, which

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.169
allows you to go where you want, or you can choose to sign up for something

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:25.300
that they call Medicare advantage, which puts you in

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:28.479
a capitated, or managed care plan, which can restrict your

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:29.479
movement.

00:05:29.479 --> 00:05:30.699
So fundamentally, if you're thinking about who's

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:37.300
regulating the quality of hospital care, Medicare has an important role to play in asking you

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:42.360
to get either joint commission accreditation, or getting accreditation directly

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.360
from the government.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.710
Correct, and they use the power the purse to get people to follow those standards, because

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.940
the Medicare business often tends to comprise a significant share of most

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:51.169
hospitals.

